@@ -1,5 +1,15 @@
 # Changelog
 
+## Unreleased
+- **Rules with triggers.** Markdown files in `.agents/rules/` (this project, up to the repository root) or in ORBIX's `rules` folder (every project) start with frontmatter naming a `trigger`: `always_on` (in every request), `model_decision` (the agent sees its description and reads it when it fits), `glob` with `globs:` (added the first time the agent reads or changes a matching file, or you @-mention one) and `manual` (only when you @-mention it by name). `AGENTS.md` and `CLAUDE.md` work as before. `/memory` lists the rules and any file that was skipped.
+- **More hook events and decisions.** New events: `SessionStart`, `PreInvocation` and `PostInvocation` (before and after each model call). Besides exit code 2, a hook can print JSON: `{"decision":"block","reason":"…"}` blocks, `"allow"` passes, `"continue"` keeps a Stop going, and `{"additionalContext":"…"}` adds a note for the agent. Existing hook files keep working.
+- **Projects with several folders.** Add extra folders to a project from its ⋯ menu in the sidebar (**Add a folder to this project…**; **Remove folder …** keeps the files). The agent reads, searches and edits in every project folder with the same approvals as the main folder, and the system prompt lists them; anything outside all of them still asks. Projects saved before load unchanged. A run still makes its worktree from the main folder's Git repository; in a run the extra folders are read-only (a write there asks every time and is not part of the changes you apply).
+- **Scheduled runs.** A task can run by itself at set times: once, every N minutes or hours, daily, or on weekdays at HH:MM. Make one in Runs (Ctrl+2) → **New schedule…**, or type `/schedule daily 09:00 run the tests and fix failures` in a conversation (`--commands` allows commands, `--direct` skips the plan). The list shows the next time and the last result, with on/off, **Run now** and **Delete**.
+  - Each time starts an ordinary run in its own worktree. It runs **unattended**: its plan is approved with the scope you chose when you made the schedule, and anything that would ask you (a command outside that scope, a destructive command, a file outside the worktree) is denied and shown in the run's timeline. The changes wait for you in Review; nothing is applied for you. Acting on the run yourself (approve, feedback, resume) makes it ask you again.
+  - Schedules run while ORBIX is open. A time missed while it was closed runs once when ORBIX starts, then the schedule continues. A time that comes while the last run is still working is skipped.
+- **Apply part of a run.** Apply changes… lists the changed files with a box each, all ticked. In the Changes tab you can also untick single hunks of a file. The chosen parts go to your branch as one commit; the rest stays in the run's worktree, the run stays open, and the Changes tab shows only what is still to apply. A conflict, or an uncommitted edit of yours to a chosen file, changes nothing. The run's log records what was applied, part by part.
+- **Comments on diff lines.** Click a line number in a run's Changes tab to comment on that line, Shift+click for a range. The comment shows `file:line`, the line is marked in the diff, and the agent gets the file, the line numbers and the quoted lines.
+
 ## 3.4.1 (2026-11-20)
 - **Turbo asks before destructive commands.** In every mode, including Turbo, a command that deletes folders recursively, pushes, publishes, rewrites history or changes the system waits for your OK. (3.4.0 already said so; now it is true outside runs as well.) Turbo still reads and writes outside the project folder without asking, and the Turbo confirmation says so.
 - **Easier to read.** Replies get a calmer rhythm: clearer headings and lists, quiet inline code, file paths that stand out (click one to open it), one clean frame around code blocks (the double border is gone), tables that highlight the row under the pointer, and the time, copy and model under every reply.
